UFS synchronous-init host regression tests
========================================

Run without a kernel build or device. Python 3, a C11 host compiler, and git
(or an explicitly supplied fixed-baseline source export) are required.

  make -C /home/ssx/android-kernel/tools/testing/selftests/ufs run_tests \
    SRC=/home/ssx/android-kernel \
    OUT=/mnt/e/LG/ufs-release-gate-re61k1hh/selftests/normal HOSTCC=cc

  make -C /home/ssx/android-kernel/tools/testing/selftests/ufs run_tests \
    SRC=/home/ssx/android-kernel \
    OUT=/mnt/e/LG/ufs-release-gate-re61k1hh/selftests/sanitized \
    HOSTCC=cc SANITIZE=1

Equivalent direct invocation (supports isolated Python):

  python3 -I /home/ssx/android-kernel/tools/testing/selftests/ufs/test_sync_init.py \
    --src /home/ssx/android-kernel --out /absolute/external/output --hostcc cc

Both --src and --out are mandatory. Output must be outside the source tree.
HOSTCC can include compiler arguments. No candidate path, temporary directory,
HEAD baseline, patch generation, source modification, fetch, or device access
is built into the runner. Make propagates Python, extraction, compiler,
assertion, and sanitizer failures. Sanitizer mode enables ASan+UBSan and makes
both fatal, with non-PIE host executables for reliable ASan shadow placement.

What actually executes
----------------------
Each run reads the selected current working-tree source, lexically masks C
comments/strings for brace matching, and fails on missing/ambiguous function
or slice boundaries. It writes generated C, host binaries, and an extraction
SHA-256 manifest only under OUT. Shim fixtures contain stubs and assertions;
the tested production bodies are inserted at ACTUAL_SOURCE, not reimplemented
as a Python lifecycle model. Reference transformation scripts are never run.

* lifecycle: complete queue_recovery, init_handoff, scan_handoff, hold_all;
  512 event/fatal combinations, sticky INIT failure even after error fields
  clear, LIVE EH preservation, independent scan handoff and hold unwind.
* scan: complete async_scan/card_detect_handler and handoff/recovery functions;
  deterministic actual EH queue insertion at the probe-return boundary, with
  EH and PM flags both set, tests caller-owned readiness on success/failure;
  also tests async hold failure. Probe itself is a stub. These cases DO NOT
  assert PM balance under arbitrary EH/PM interleavings: existing real probe
  PM puts depend on !EH && !pm_op_in_progress. This suite claims handoff
  independence only, not that concurrent probe PM behavior was changed.
* hibern8: three complete enter/exit functions; 12 enter + 12 exit executions,
  INIT/STOPPING errors and LINERESET, and existing LIVE BUG policy. BUG is
  counted rather than crashing the host. Link recovery and UIC are stubs.
* tm: ONLY the real acquisition/hold/failure-unwind prefix of issue_tm_cmd,
  through the first host_lock acquisition. Successful completion is stubbed;
  no claim to test TM descriptors, interrupts, timeout, or command execution.
* pm: real init suffix from IRQ registration to the final return, including
  all original failure labels, plus COMPLETE platform and PCI probe callers.
  Earlier init setup and hardware operations are stubbed. 75 cases cover
  platform PM-disabled normal probe, PCI core's preexisting reference, !PM,
  request-IRQ/scsi-add/enable/workqueue failures, and INIT EH injection during
  IRQ registration, HBA enable, PM get, and command-log init. Checks sticky
  failure, publication order, usage balance, IRQ synchronization/free before
  cancellation, and no publication on failure. PM-disabled get_sync returns
  -EACCES, !PM get_sync returns -ENOSYS, enabled get_sync returns +1; noresume
  takes a reference without resuming. These are explicit host PM contracts,
  NOT executions of Linux PM core. Async scan completion is modeled by one
  final put only in the successful PM test; full probe_hba is not executed.

The source-order check additionally requires EH work initialization before
request_irq, enable, handoff, and async publication, and no synchronous goto
after the LIVE commit. This is a structural assertion, not runtime coverage
of the omitted init prefix. No test substitutes for kernel/device validation.

Fixed normal-remove baseline and shallow repositories
----------------------------------------------------
Normal ufshcd_remove is compared byte-for-byte against the immutable commit
42758fbbe770918f9c654e78791e3893fa7dbef4, never against the tree's own HEAD.
The short form 42758 is ambiguous in this repository. The git object exists
in the validated shallow tree. If absent elsewhere, the test FAILS and never
fetches. Export drivers/scsi/ufs/ufshcd.c from that exact commit in a trusted
repository, transfer it, and supply BASELINE_SOURCE=/absolute/export to make
(or --baseline-source to Python). The caller is responsible for provenance
of such an export; the test cannot independently authenticate an arbitrary
file. It still compares the actual remove function.

Validated results
-----------------
Logs and generated C are under
/mnt/e/LG/ufs-release-gate-re61k1hh/selftests.
normal.log and sanitized.log: all five suites pass against final current
source after the separate init get_sync -> get_noresume fix.
negative-old.log: unmodified 7bf7d707f915 source fails the PM-disabled normal
platform probe success assertion, after the other four suites pass; make=2.
negative-hostcc.log: HOSTCC=false fails, make=2.
negative-extraction.log: nonexistent source fails, make=2.
negative-missing-function.log: a separate external fixture with the handoff
function definition renamed fails actual-function extraction, make=2.
negative-missing-args.log: missing SRC/OUT fails, make=2.
pre-pm.log is an earlier development compile log, not a release result.

To reproduce the old-source negative control without changing the worktree,
export the three drivers/scsi/ufs/{ufshcd,ufshcd-pltfrm,ufshcd-pci}.c files from
7bf7d707f915 into a new external directory preserving their paths. Invoke
make with SRC pointing there, an independent OUT, and BASELINE_SOURCE set to
the trusted fixed-baseline export above. The expected outcome is nonzero.
